{
	title: 'Slash commands as files',
	spec: `Every slash command is one file in src/host/commands/, named like the command (cd.ts, help.ts, close.ts), found by listing the directory, so adding a command touches nothing else. Contract and rules in tasks/w4/forms.md (Commands): description, category, optional help(args), complete(args) and run(args, answers). Commands run on the host, and completion is a protocol request, "so /cd ~/projec<Tab> completes against the host's files for terminal, web and remote clients alike. /cd lists directories sorted, so an exact match (~/.hal) comes before its extensions (~/.hal-fresh) and is selected first." A command asks by returning a question (a durable form); the answer re-runs it with answers, so nothing waits in memory. Ship /help (grouped by category, /help <name> for detail) and /cd (with 'not found, create? (Y/n)'; a change reports 'Directory changed: <old> → <new>', like /rename). A typed command is recorded with who typed it (see Provenance).

An idle session stays idle through bare /login method selection, concurrent ChatGPT device-code logins and their outputs, in the current state, tab list and reopened snapshot; a genuinely active model turn keeps its own state. The working tab indicator must not remain from an orphan command answer. When reconstructing from sparse history marks, the last answer may belong to an older command question whose record was displaced by a newer question: an answer whose question is absent must not count as turn work. Command questions and outputs never manufacture an unfinished turn. Completion events keep full replacement strings in items and may carry an aligned descriptions string list supplied by the command on the host; clients validate string elements and equal list lengths. Older events without descriptions remain valid.`,
	status: 'done',
	notes: [
		"Shape: src/host/commands.ts lists src/host/commands/*.ts on every call (no cache; require caches modules) and reads each file's `command` export (a SlashCommand). run(args, answers, ctx) and complete(args, ctx) take a third Context { sessionId, cwd, setCwd } beyond the forms.md contract; run returns a Reply { say?, error?, ask? } (like synthetic.ts) and may be async. commands.home() and commands.dir() are overridable for tests.",
		"Dispatch: host.submit runs text matching /^\\/[a-z][a-z0-9-]*(\\s|$)/ as a command at once, whatever the state (busy included, never via the inbox). An unknown name is refused ('unknown command'); '/tmp/x is broken' is a prompt. queue is ignored for commands.",
		'History: `command` { text, from?, command? (client id, for resend detection in host.submitted) } and `output` { text, error? } records; question gets `from: { command, args }` when a command asked; answer gets `cancelled: true` when Escape dismissed a command question. replay.toMessages skips command/output: the model is not told about /cd yet (a later task may want a <meta> note about cwd changes). Events: command, output, meta (session metadata changed), completions (sender only).',
		"State: a command is not a turn, and its question is not a session state. The session state belongs to the turn alone; a command's open question is data beside it, like the inbox: it opens in any state (a turn blocked on a login is exactly when /login asks, twice: method, then code), and opening, answering or dismissing it never changes the state. Do not derive the live state from history after a command (an old host.settle did; it overwrote the host's in-memory `blocked: login` with `running`, so /login's second question was refused as busy). states.fromHistory: blocked only on a turn's open question; replay.withoutCommands drops command records, command questions and their answers. forms.open: at most one question is open; a turn end closes a turn's question, never a command's. A command asking while a turn's question is open outputs an error; one asking while a command's is open replaces it (slash.dismiss records a cancelled answer), and so does a turn that asks. The question event carries `command: true` and `streaming` and folds as an aside (task rk) that never ends the live turn; transcript.question opens a command's question whatever the state. pages marks keep the last answer apart from turn ends (`answer`; marks with the old `close` are rebuilt).",
		'Test the boundary rather than restating a result: command questions leave idle/paused/error/running state unchanged, while a blocked login still accepts the entire bare /login method-then-code flow. A separate test of turn questions after an already answered command question adds no coverage over those two behaviors; keep one integrated login scenario rather than duplicating the whole OAuth setup.',
		'Escape on a command question (the pause command) records a cancelled answer instead of pausing; the command does not re-run.',
		"Completion: `complete` { sessionId, text } -> `completions` { text, items } with full replacement texts ('/cd ', '/cd ~/projects/'). common/completion.ts applies them (one replaces, several extend to their common prefix and list last parts as a notice); clients drop an answer if the prompt changed since. Terminal and web complete only with the cursor at the end.",
		"Provenance: submit has optional `from` (sender session id), recorded on command records and shown as '(sent from <id>)'; prompts don't carry it yet. Nothing checks `from`: a later task that lets sessions send to each other must set it host-side, not trust the client.",
		"Transcript: where command/output events go beside a live turn is decided by task rk (after the round's written blocks, before the one still streaming); fold and snapshot agree.",
		"Sparse pages.marks keeps the last answer and last question independently: a second bare /login can write a new method question while the preceding method answer's question is no longer in the marks. replay.withoutCommands used to mistake that orphan answer for a turn, tabs.observe published a running tab mark at question time, and later /login output emitted no tabs update. Ignore answers without their question when deriving turn state; regression runs two overlapping device logins and checks the latest tabs event, not just status.stateOf (which recomputes correctly after the next answer).",
		"Reply's after-the-turn callback is named after, not then: an object with a then field is a thenable, which await would call (unicorn/no-thenable).",
		"Completion labels cut where the items' shared part last breaks (space or /), not at each item's last space: invariant: a multi-word argument (a session name for /go) is listed whole, a path by its last part. Labels once showed only a name's last word ('monitoring', '(fork)')."
	],
	needs: ['w4']
}
